{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>For every firstborn is Mine, etc.</b> I ask one question, and this is its form: if HaShem gave the Levites distinction and authority through this appointment, for He set them apart from the congregation of Israel to stand and minister before Him — if so, what is the meaning of what He said, ״כִּי לִי כָּל בְּכוֹר בְּיוֹם הַכֹּתִי״, etc.? Did HaShem give the firstborn this authority at that time? Was not the service performed by the firstborn from days of old? And furthermore, on the day that He struck every firstborn He gave the firstborn no authority at all — on the contrary, He imposed upon them the yoke of redemption and obligated them to give five shekels, because He saved them from the plague of the firstborn. If so, what connection is there between the sanctification of the firstborn and the sanctification of the Levites? For to the Levites He gave distinction and authority, but upon the firstborn He imposed the yoke of redemption. And if we say that He imposed servitude upon the Levites in that they would stand before Aaron and minister to him, as it is written ״נְתוּנִים נְתוּנִים הֵמָּה לוֹ״, and on this basis it is perfectly said ״כִּי לִי כָּל בְּכוֹר״ etc. — to say that at that time justice warranted imposing this servitude upon them — nevertheless it is difficult: why would HaShem impose this servitude upon the Levites? Is it because they were not disqualified through the golden calf that He would impose this servitude upon them?" "Truly, my heart does not think so, for certainly HaShem gave the Levites authority and not servitude, as it is written (Numbers 16:9): ״הַמְעַט מִכֶּם כִּי הִבְדִּיל ה׳״, etc. And formerly this service belonged to the firstborn, and they were disqualified through the golden calf, and it was given to the Levites. But it was revealed before Him, blessed be He, that even though every firstborn would give a Levite in his place, nevertheless in the end 273 firstborn would remain for whom no Levites could be provided in their stead, and they would require redemption of five shekels per head. And there is room here for a firstborn to lodge a complaint and say: Is it because there is no Levite available for me that I must give five shekels? Was it not enough that He penalized me by disqualifying me from the service — why must I also give my replacement of five shekels? Of this He said: ״כִּי לִי כָּל בְּכוֹר בְּיוֹם הַכֹּתִי״, etc. — therefore I have a claim against them both from then and also now, to impose upon them a twofold penalty: one on account of the old debt I hold against them, that I saved them from death; and one on account of the fact that they sinned with the golden calf. And this interpretation is sound from the standpoint of the matter."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:39:35.232761222Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}